{"author":"RebarBotNava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"fields":{"description":"# Jira Server / Data Center support via a Jira-family seam (not a forked second adapter)\n\n## Context / Problem\n\nCMS and similar federal clients run **self-hosted Jira Data Center**, which rebar cannot reconcile against.\nDC differs from Cloud on three axes: REST v2 with wiki-markup/plain-text bodies instead of v3 + ADF, Personal\nAccess Token bearer auth instead of the ACLI subprocess, and name-based user identity instead of `accountId`.\n\nGitHub PR #120 proposed a second adapter package for this. It rides ADR 0035's `Backend` port correctly, but it\nreaches into `adapters/jira/` privates (`_map_local_to_jira_fields`, `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS`,\n`_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY`, `_sanitize_label`, `_RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK`, `classify_probe_response`) and forks\n`map_fields_to_remote` as a verbatim copy of `adapters/jira/backend.py:42-72` with one line changed. That\ncreates a de facto \"Jira family\" layer with no contract: `adapters/jira/` becomes simultaneously a concrete\nbackend and a shared library, with nothing pinning what is shared and nothing stopping a Cloud change from\nbreaking DC.\n\n## Approach\n\nMake the Jira family an **explicit sub-seam** rather than an accident, in an order that never puts the\nlive-validated Cloud path at risk.\n\n1. **Freeze Cloud first.** Extend the existing held-out characterization tests\n   (`tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py`) to pin the current outputs of every unit\n   about to move, so any behavior change during extraction fails a pinned oracle.\n2. **Extract a `adapters/jira_family/` shared layer** — and re-point the Cloud adapter at it. Cloud's ACLI\n   transport is not touched at all.\n\n   **Only non-location-pinned symbols may physically move.** ADR 0035 §(a) pins `adf.py`,\n   `outbound_fields.py`, and `comment_limits.py` as **Jira-coupled *and* location-pinned**: they are loaded by\n   filename relative to `_PACKAGE_DIR` (via `_loader.lazy_load(` / `_load_sibling_keyed(` /\n   `spec_from_file_location(`), so physically moving them breaks the dynamic loader unless the loader is taught\n   the new sub-package in the same change — deferred to ADR 0035's Phase 2, explicitly **out of scope here**.\n   **Location-pinned means \"cannot change path\" — NOT \"cannot be edited.\"** Editing a pinned module's\n   *contents* (e.g. replacing a dict literal with an import) is safe and is how the pinned units get\n   single-sourced; only *relocating* the file breaks the loader.\n\n   **Move by SYMBOL, not by file.** `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py` cannot relocate wholesale: it imports\n   `fit_text_to_adf_limit` from the pinned `adf.py` (used by `_sanitize_description`) and\n   `truncate_comment_body` / `_JIRA_COMMENT_MAX_CHARS` from the pinned `comment_limits.py` (used by\n   `_sanitize_comment`). Moving the whole file would point `jira_family/` at `adapters/jira/` — the exact\n   dependency inversion the import contract in §6 forbids. So the file is split by symbol, and the two coupled\n   sanitizers are **parameterized by the contracts this epic already introduces** rather than importing a\n   vendor module: `_sanitize_description` takes the `RichTextCodec` (its `fit_text_to_adf_limit` call *is* the\n   rich-text seam), and comment truncation takes the vendor's comment-limit/truncation contract. Cloud\n   constructs those contract objects in `adapters/jira/` from its pinned `adf.py` / `comment_limits.py`; DC\n   constructs wiki-markup/plain-text equivalents. `jira_family/` imports neither.\n\n   Concretely, of the six symbols PR #120 reached for:\n   - **Movable to `jira_family/` in this epic:** `_sanitize_label`, `_sanitize_summary`, `InvalidLabelError`,\n     and `_RELATION_TO_JIRA_LINK` (all in `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py`, not location-pinned and not\n     ADF/limit-coupled), `classify_probe_response` (`adapters/jira/probe.py`, not location-pinned), and the new\n     `RichTextCodec` / `UserIdentityModel` contract objects.\n   - **Moves to `jira_family/` only in contract-parameterized form:** `_sanitize_description` and\n     `_sanitize_comment` — same observable behavior, but the ADF/limit dependency arrives as an injected\n     contract instead of a direct import of a pinned module.\n\n   - **Stays put (path-wise):** `_map_local_to_jira_fields` in the pinned `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py`,\n     and the ADF encoder in the pinned `adapters/jira/adf.py`. These are shared **by dependency-inverting\n     instead of moving**: the pinned module is parameterized by the `jira_family` contracts (it imports *from*\n     `jira_family/`, never the reverse), so DC reuses one implementation without any file relocating and\n     without the loader changing. Any story that would *move* a pinned file must instead stop and record the\n     blocker.\n\n   One more unit needs parameterizing rather than sharing verbatim: `classify_probe_response` hardcodes\n   `RESOLVED_STATUS_NAMES = frozenset({\"Resolved\", \"Done\", \"Cancelled\"})`. Those are Cloud/DIG workflow state\n   names; a self-hosted DC workflow can name its resolved states anything. So the classifier moves to\n   `jira_family/` taking the resolved-status set **as a parameter**, with Cloud passing today's frozenset\n   (pinned unchanged by J1's oracle) and DC passing its own configured set. Sharing it with the Cloud names\n   baked in would silently misclassify a DC issue as `PRESENT_FILTERED` when it is in fact resolved.\n   The DC set is **operator-configured** via a `reconciler.resolved_statuses` config key (a list of workflow\n   state names), defaulting to the same `{Resolved, Done, Cancelled}` when unset so an operator whose workflow\n   uses the conventional names configures nothing. Cloud continues to pass its own frozenset, so Cloud is not\n   made configurable by this change.\n\n   **The value maps are a special case — they are ALREADY duplicated, and this epic must not preserve the\n   duplication.** There are today **three** independently-referenced copies of the local→Jira status map (and\n   two of the priority map):\n   - `adapters/jira/jira_fields.py`: `_LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA` / `_LOCAL_PRIORITY_TO_JIRA` — read **only** by the\n     ACLI transport (`acli.py:69-70,143,171`; `acli_cli_ops.py:34,343,354`).\n   - `adapters/jira/outbound_fields.py` (pinned): `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS` / `_LOCAL_TO_JIRA_PRIORITY` — read by\n     the `Backend` port via `backend.py`'s `map_fields_to_remote`.\n   - `rebar_reconciler/config.py:37`: `local_to_jira_status` — the engine-level config copy.\n\n   They have **already drifted**: `outbound_fields` and `config.py` both carry a `\"deleted\": \"Done\"` entry the\n   `jira_fields` copy lacks. So simply moving the `jira_fields` copy to `jira_family/` would leave Cloud's\n   backend still reading a second copy — the exact \"two places\" the epic exists to eliminate. This epic\n   consolidates the two `adapters/` copies; `config.py`'s is folded in by bug `fe15` (its call sites are\n   engine-level config, a wider blast radius than this epic's seam).\n\n   **Resolution: `adapters/jira_family/` holds the ONE definition of each value map. Both current copies are\n   deleted and replaced by imports from it** — `jira_fields.py` (whose map literals move out, the file itself\n   staying put for its ADF/limit-coupled sanitizers) and `outbound_fields.py` (pinned, so it stays at its path\n   but its dict literals become an import). After this,\n   grep finds exactly one dict literal per map, reachable from the ACLI transport, the Cloud backend port, and\n   the DC backend alike.\n\n   The `deleted`-key drift **must be decided in J2**, because a single unified dict cannot preserve two\n   callers that currently disagree. J2 adopts `\"deleted\": \"Done\"` — the value **two of the three** existing\n   copies already use (`outbound_fields._LOCAL_TO_JIRA_STATUS` and `config.py:37`'s `local_to_jira_status`).\n   The ACLI-side omission produces `\"Deleted\"` via a `.title()` fallback, which is not a state in the live DIG\n   workflow, so Jira would reject that transition: a latent bug, not behavior to preserve. This is the one\n   declared observable change in an otherwise behavior-preserving extraction, pinned by its own test. Bug\n   `fe15-3bc4-ed70-4b61` (`controlled-ornate-honeybee`) keeps the remainder: the missing ACLI-side\n   characterization pins and folding `config.py`'s third copy into the single definition.\n3. **Pin the two real axes of variation as contracts**: a `RichTextCodec` (ADF for Cloud, wiki/plain text for\n   DC) and a `UserIdentityModel` (`accountId` for Cloud, `name` for DC). Cloud is parameterized by its existing\n   behavior; DC is a different configuration, not a fork.\n4. **DC transport builds on the maintained `pycontribs/jira` library**, gated behind a `[jira-datacenter]`\n   extra and imported lazily, so the engine's `dependencies = []` contract and the battle-tested Cloud path are\n   both untouched.\n5. **Verify against a real Jira.** Atlassian stopped issuing DC trial licenses on 30 March 2026, so the oracle\n   is the Jira 8.17.1 test instance the pycontribs/jira project uses, reachable at\n   `http://localhost:2990/jira` — the same substrate that project validates itself against.\n\n   **It is BUILT, not pulled — corrected against the registry.** An earlier version of this step said to\n   `docker run -dit -p 2990:2990 pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1` and called it a pinned *pullable* image.\n   That is false: `docker pull` returns \"repository does not exist\", the Docker Hub API returns\n   `object not found`, and the `pycontribs` namespace publishes only seven OS base images. The tag is a LOCAL\n   BUILD NAME — pycontribs/jira ships `docker/jira-test-image/Dockerfile` and its README's own instruction is\n   `docker build -t pycontribs/jira-test-image:8.17.1 docker/jira-test-image`. Its base,\n   `addono/jira-software-standalone`, IS pullable and the digest pycontribs pins resolves. J5\n   [rebar:c97f-90ae-4976-47c7] therefore vendors a small Dockerfile pinned to that base digest; the mechanism\n   and its three load-bearing details (`-DskipAllPrompts=true`, the TTY requirement, 8.x-only) live there.\n\n   Two consequences worth recording at epic level because they shape what this oracle can promise: the image\n   does NOT contain Jira — `atlas-run` downloads it from `maven.atlassian.com` at first start, so egress to\n   Atlassian is a hard runtime dependency and **pinning the image digest does not pin Jira itself** — and the\n   base is `linux/amd64`, so an arm64 runner emulates it and starts far slower than upstream's quoted 3-5\n   minutes. Jira 8.17.1 is ≥ 8.14, the release that introduced Personal Access Tokens, so PAT\n   bearer auth is exercised for real. J5 [rebar:c97f-90ae-4976-47c7] owns the harness and records the\n   version-drift limitation (8.17.1 is older than a client such as CMS is likely running, so it proves REST v2\n   protocol semantics, not deployment-specific workflow/field/permission behavior — tracked separately as\n   `9895-ac7d-4276-4f08`).\n6. **Keep the new boundary structurally enforced, not conventional.** The drift this epic exists to prevent can\n   recur the moment a contributor adds Cloud-specific logic to the shared layer. So the seam ships with a\n   machine-checked import contract (an `import-linter` contract in `pyproject.toml`, or an equivalent CI grep\n   check): only `adapters/jira/` and `adapters/jira_datacenter/` may import `adapters/jira_family/`, and\n   `jira_family/` may import neither of them.\n7. **Security posture for the DC connection.** Self-hosted DC is frequently served over plain HTTP inside\n   corporate networks, which would put ticket bodies and the PAT on the wire in cleartext. The DC backend\n   therefore **requires `https`** for `reconciler.base_url` and rejects a non-TLS URL unless an explicit\n   `allow_insecure = true` override is set (which logs a warning). Operator docs state the least-privilege\n   expectation: a **dedicated service account** whose PAT is scoped to only the projects rebar reconciles\n   (Browse Projects / Create Issues / Edit Issues / Add Comments / Link Issues on those projects), never an\n   admin-level token.\n\n   **The PAT is env-only, never file-config.** Like Cloud's `JIRA_API_TOKEN`, the DC PAT is read from a\n   `JIRA_PAT` environment variable and is NOT accepted from `[tool.rebar.jira]` in a committed config file, so\n   the credential cannot be checked into a repo by accident. The DC transport never logs the token, and a\n   missing `JIRA_PAT` raises a config error naming the variable rather than falling back to anonymous access.\n\n   **Certificate verification stays ON.** Self-hosted DC commonly presents a self-signed or internal-CA\n   certificate, and the tempting fix — disabling verification — turns the `https` requirement into theatre. So\n   the DC transport never sets `verify=False`: an operator with an internal CA points rebar at their CA bundle\n   (a `ca_bundle` config key / the standard `REQUESTS_CA_BUNDLE` env var honoured by the underlying client), and\n   a verification failure surfaces as a clear error naming the CA-bundle setting rather than being silently\n   downgraded. `allow_insecure` governs the URL *scheme* only; it does NOT relax certificate checking.\n\n   **The J5 harness is the intended `allow_insecure` case, not an exception to it.** The Dockerized Jira serves\n   plain `http://localhost:2990/jira`, so every live test against it sets `allow_insecure = true` explicitly and\n   asserts the warning is emitted. That is deliberate: it means the loopback-only test path exercises the\n   override rather than bypassing the validator, and the validator's reject-by-default branch is covered by a\n   unit test with a non-loopback `http://` URL. The override is **not** narrowed to loopback — an operator on a\n   private network is a real use case — but the warning text names the cleartext risk.\n\n**Provenance decision.** DC and Cloud share the single `jira` creation channel, `jira-` local-id prefix, and\n`jira_key` binding; deployments are disambiguated by the `RemoteRef.instance` field that ADR 0035 §(d) already\ndefines for exactly this. They are the same vendor. Generalizing the store vocabulary is epic `be74`'s work,\nwhere a genuinely different vendor (GitHub) makes it necessary; doing it here would mean a store migration for\nno user-visible benefit.\n\n**Alternative rejected: PR #120's separate adapter package.** Two copies of the field maps and sanitizers drift,\nand cross-adapter private imports are a dependency direction ADR 0035 never sanctioned.\n\n**Alternative rejected: a hand-rolled stdlib REST client.** PR #120's client is competent, but pycontribs/jira\nalready absorbs DC-vs-Cloud auth, pagination, and payload quirks that thousands of users have found. Confining\nit to an opt-in extra keeps the cost off every rebar install.\n\n**Alternative rejected: `atlassian-python-api`.** It was surveyed in ADR 0004 and does support DC with PAT\nauth, but `pycontribs/jira` is the better fit here for two reasons: it ships the Dockerized-Jira-SDK test\nharness pattern this epic adopts as its verification oracle (its own `tests/conftest.py`), and its\n`JIRA(server=…, token_auth=…)` surface covers DC-vs-Cloud divergence behind one client object. Either would\nwork; picking one and confining it to an opt-in extra is what matters.\n\n**Alternative rejected: generalizing `jira_key` / the `jira-` prefix / the channel vocabulary now.** That is\nepic `be74`'s spine and touches 50+ binding-store sites; pulling it forward delays the CMS delivery for no\nbehavioral gain.\n\n**Alternative rejected: moving the location-pinned modules now.** Relocating `outbound_fields.py` / `adf.py`\ninto `jira_family/` would be the tidier file layout, but it requires teaching the dynamic loader the new\nsub-package and re-targeting every `mock.patch(\"rebar_reconciler.<mod>.<attr>\")` site in the suite (ADR 0035\n§(a)–(b)). That is ADR 0035's Phase 2; doing it here would couple a live-Cloud-risking refactor to a delivery\nepic for no DC-visible benefit. Dependency inversion gets the same single-implementation guarantee at a\nfraction of the risk.\n\n## Scope\n\n- `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/jira_family/` (new), `adapters/jira/`, `adapters/jira_datacenter/` (new).\n- `src/rebar/_config_schema.py` — widen `reconciler.backend` (**owned by J7**), add the TLS/`allow_insecure`\n  validator and the `ca_bundle` + `resolved_statuses` keys (**owned by J6**). Split deliberately: J6 lands the\n  DC connection settings with the transport that consumes them; J7 lands the backend-choice widening with the\n  assembly that makes the choice valid.\n- `pyproject.toml` — `[jira-datacenter]` extra, and the import contract.\n- `tests/unit/rebar_reconciler/test_backend_characterization.py`, `test_backend_contract.py`.\n- A new ADR amending ADR 0035.\n- **Out of scope:** relocating any location-pinned module (`adf.py`, `outbound_fields.py`,\n  `comment_limits.py`) or touching `_loader.py` — ADR 0035 Phase 2 owns that.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n\n- [x] every child story is closed and its change is `Verified +1` on Gerrit\n- [x] `test_backend_contract.py`'s backend fixture includes `jira-datacenter` and the suite passes for it\n- [x] an ADR records the Jira-family sub-seam, the shared `jira` provenance identity, and the DC EOL horizon\n- [x] [operator-attested] rebar reconciles bidirectionally against the Dockerized Jira SDK instance at\n      `localhost:2990`, selected by `reconciler.backend = \"jira-datacenter\"` with `reconciler.base_url`\n      pointing at that instance: at least one issue created in DC appears in the local store after an inbound\n      pass, and one local edit appears on the DC issue after an outbound pass, with zero unhandled exceptions\n      in the reconcile log\n- [x] No SHARED mapping logic exists in two places, and each VARYING unit has exactly one shared CONTRACT:\n      (a) the field/value maps, the label sanitizer, the link-relation vocabulary, and the probe classifier\n      each have exactly one implementation in `adapters/jira_family/`, imported by both backends (or, for\n      the location-pinned modules, consumed parameterized by a `jira_family` contract); and (b) the\n      rich-text codec and the user-identity model each have exactly one shared CONTRACT in\n      `adapters/jira_family/` with one implementation PER DEPLOYMENT. `adapters/jira_datacenter/` defines no\n      copy of any unit in (a). Cross-imports between `adapters/jira/` and `adapters/jira_datacenter/` are\n      forbidden and machine-checked, failing CI on a violation.\n      **AMENDED — the original wording required \"exactly one implementation reachable by both backends\" for\n      ALL SIX units including the codec and the identity model. That prescribes an outcome that would be\n      WRONG if achieved:** Cloud is ADF and DC is wiki/plain text; Cloud identifies users by `accountId`\n      and DC by `name`. Two implementations of one pinned contract IS the correct design\n      (`jira_family/rich_text.py:36 RichTextCodec` → `WikiTextCodec` / `AdfCodec`;\n      `jira_family/identity_model.py:42 UserIdentityModel` → `NameIdentity` / `AccountIdIdentity`). Two\n      further corrections folded in, both recorded in this epic's AC-verification comment: the create-path\n      field map legitimately exists twice (`jira_datacenter/backend.py:53 _map_local_to_dc_fields`, because\n      Cloud's version lazy-loads the location-pinned `adf.py` BY FILE PATH — a coupling DC must not carry),\n      so the \"defines none of its own\" clause is scoped to the units in (a); and the enforcement clause is\n      narrowed to what the checker actually does — it forbids cross-IMPORTS and pins one dict literal per\n      value map, but cannot prevent a re-DEFINITION, so four of the six units rest on convention plus\n      review. The substance was verified: cross-imports are empty on `gerrit/main`, the boundary checker\n      fires on 11 forbidden edges and stays silent on 4 permitted ones, and the value-map single-sourcing\n      is asserted by object IDENTITY (`... is LOCAL_STATUS_TO_JIRA`), not equality\n- [x] The Cloud adapter's observable behavior is unchanged: characterization oracles pinning every unit that\n      moves or is re-pointed were committed **before** the extraction change was pushed for review (the oracle\n      change's Gerrit number recorded on the extraction story), and the full oracle set passes against the\n      post-extraction Cloud adapter\n- [x] `JiraDataCenterBackend` is exercised by the backend-agnostic contract suite, not only by adapter-local tests.\n- [x] Installing rebar without the `[jira-datacenter]` extra pulls in no new dependency — `pip install\n      nava-rebar` then `pip list` shows no `jira` package and `import rebar` still works — **and the existing\n      clean-core CI check enforces it**: `jira` is added to the absent-module list in\n      `.github/workflows/_optionality.yml` (today it checks only `langchain`, `langgraph`, `anthropic`,\n      `inspect_ai`, `opentelemetry`, so without this the guarantee has no CI teeth).\n- [x] No location-pinned module (`adf.py`, `outbound_fields.py`, `comment_limits.py`) changed path, and\n      `_loader.py` is untouched — verified by `git diff --stat origin/main` on the landed stack (their\n      *contents* may change to import from `jira_family/`; only a rename is forbidden)\n- [x] Exactly one dict literal defines the local→Jira status map and one the priority map **within\n      `adapters/`** — the pre-existing `jira_fields` / `outbound_fields` duplicate pair is gone, and the ACLI\n      transport, the Cloud backend port, and the DC backend all read the single `jira_family` definition\n      (verified by grep for the map literals under `src/rebar/_engine/rebar_reconciler/adapters/`).\n      `rebar_reconciler/config.py`'s third copy is the ONE knowingly-remaining literal outside `adapters/`,\n      deferred to bug `fe15-3bc4-ed70-4b61` because its call sites are engine-level config with a wider blast\n      radius than this seam — so the grep is scoped to `adapters/`, not the whole engine\n- [x] The DC backend rejects a non-`https` `reconciler.base_url` unless `allow_insecure = true` is set, warns\n      when the override is used, and never disables TLS certificate verification\n- [x] The operator docs state the least-privilege service-account/PAT scope and how to point rebar at an\n      internal CA bundle\n- [x] `reconciler.resolved_statuses` exists as a validated config key (list of workflow state names), defaults\n      to `{Resolved, Done, Cancelled}` when unset, and the DC absence probe classifies against it — so an\n      operator whose workflow uses non-standard resolved-state names can configure them without a code change"}},"env_id":"ade0f2ef-51cc-45a2-bb97-c09846cd3df5","event_type":"EDIT","timestamp":1785826262720033001,"uuid":"ac5a66ae-5d4b-40af-b13a-0f4e803a1543"}